From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] watcher: transform, narrow and share the informer cache

STUB: this file states the intent and the exact change. The diff is
written with the akua-apply engine implementation; until then no
MODULE.bazel references this patch.

Upstream: github.com/fluxcd/cli-utils v1.4.0 (pkg/kstatus/watcher).

Problem. DefaultStatusWatcher.Watch builds its informers with a
hardcoded NewDynamicInformerFactory (default_status_watcher.go:99);
ObjectStatusReporter.InformerFactory is the concrete
*DynamicInformerFactory; the AllowListObjectFilter runs after objects are
already in the informer cache (object_status_reporter.go:486). Every
object of every watched kind and namespace is therefore cached in full,
including metadata.managedFields and the values of Secret and ConfigMap
members. On a small server that costs memory; for Secrets it also keeps
customer secret values in the agent process.

Change.
1. DynamicInformerFactory gains `Transform cache.TransformFunc` and
   `TweakListOptions func(*metav1.ListOptions)`. NewInformer passes
   TweakListOptions to the ListWatch list and watch calls and calls
   informer.SetTransform(Transform) before the informer starts.
2. DefaultStatusWatcher gains the same two fields and copies them into
   the factory it builds in Watch, so callers keep using Watch. It also
   accepts an optional `InformerFactory`, an interface with NewInformer
   that ObjectStatusReporter.InformerFactory now holds instead of the
   concrete type. akua-apply passes a factory that hands out informers
   of its one shared cache, so ssa waits, generated-children reads and
   live status never list the same objects twice.
3. The engine supplies a Transform that removes metadata.managedFields,
   the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation,
   Secret .data and .stringData, and ConfigMap .data and .binaryData
   before an object enters the cache. kstatus never reads those fields.
4. The engine sets TweakListOptions to the label selector
   `applyset.kubernetes.io/part-of=<applyset id>` when a Placement does
   not own its namespace exclusively (connected clusters), so only
   members are listed and watched. This only affects the member kinds
   the reporter targets: generated children (ReplicaSets, Pods) are read
   on demand through ObjectStatusReporter.ClusterReader, which the engine
   backs with its own transformed ReplicaSet and Pod informers.

Follow-up: propose (1) and (2) upstream to fluxcd/cli-utils; drop this
patch once a release contains them.
